# 1. STRICT AUDIT EXECUTION RULES

Before changing anything:

- Inspect the entire existing source code and repository structure.
- Identify the actual framework, runtime, application architecture, backend architecture, and database implementation.
- Inspect all existing modules and reusable components.
- Inspect existing database schemas, migrations, indexes, services, hooks, state management, API routes, and business logic.
- Identify existing engines and their dependencies.
- Identify previously implemented features and completed phases.
- Preserve working implementations and existing architectural contracts.
- Reuse existing components instead of unnecessarily creating duplicates.
- Identify legacy code, abandoned implementations, duplicate services, and conflicting logic.
- Trace actual data flow instead of relying on component names or documentation.

IMPORTANT:

Do not assume that an existing feature works simply because:
- Its page loads.
- Its button is clickable.
- Its modal opens.
- Its API returns HTTP 200.
- Its database table exists.
- Its TypeScript compilation succeeds.
- Its test passes without verifying actual persistence and business outcomes.

Every feature must be verified through its complete execution lifecycle.

Do not introduce unnecessary rewrites, artificial abstractions, duplicate engines, duplicate database structures, or breaking changes.

Do not silently remove existing functionality.

Do not fabricate test results, database records, performance results, or production readiness claims.

